          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry your son is having this. Wheezing at night is worth raising with a clinician, especially if it keeps happening.\n\nThe emergency check found no emergency pattern in the description you gave. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations. If your son is struggling to breathe, his lips or face look blue or grey, he cannot speak in full sentences, or he is getting worse quickly, seek emergency care now.\n\nFrom the educational summary \"Asthma\" (condition-asthma), wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough that come and go are often worse at night or in the early morning. Cough alone at night can also be a sign. The guidance \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy) explains that asthma is usually confirmed with a breathing test and that a normal result on a good day does not rule it out. Only a clinician can judge whether this applies to your son.\n\nThings you could bring to a doctor or nurse:\n- When the wheezing happens, how often, and how long it lasts\n- Any cough, colds, exercise, cold air, or allergy links you have noticed\n- Whether it is getting better, the same, or worse\n\nI could not find anything in the retrieved material about other causes of night-time wheezing in children, or about what treatment is appropriate, so please ask a clinician about those.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
