          "question": "He is four.",
          "answer": "Thank you for telling me his age. The emergency check still found no emergency pattern. Please keep the safety advice from before: if he is struggling to breathe, his lips or face look blue or grey, he cannot speak in full sentences, or he is getting worse quickly, seek emergency care now.\n\nThe retrieved material does not cover children under five. The asthma summary (\"Asthma\", condition-asthma) describes wheeze and cough that are often worse at night or in the early morning. The guidance \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy) describes the usual test, spirometry, which measures how air moves out of the lungs. It does not say how that test applies to a four-year-old, so I cannot tell you what testing would be used for him.\n\nBecause he is four, it is worth asking a doctor or nurse directly how young children with night-time wheeze are assessed. You could also bring the notes from before: when the wheezing happens, how often, any triggers such as colds, exercise, or cold air, and whether it is getting better or worse.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
